{:now [{:claim "The chord is the sine"
        :modern "Ptolemy's chord of an arc in a circle of diameter 120 is 120 times the sine of half the arc; his half-arc and sum theorems are the half-angle and addition formulas."
        :tex "\\operatorname{crd}(2a)=2R\\sin a,\\qquad \\sin(\\alpha+\\beta)=\\sin\\alpha\\cos\\beta+\\cos\\alpha\\sin\\beta"}
       {:claim "Ptolemy's theorem is complex multiplication in disguise"
        :modern "For any four points of the complex plane, (a-c)(b-d) = (a-b)(c-d) + (a-d)(b-c); taking absolute values gives Ptolemy's inequality, with equality exactly for concyclic points in order."
        :tex "|a-c|\\,|b-d|\\le|a-b|\\,|c-d|+|a-d|\\,|b-c|"}
       {:claim "Epicycles are a Fourier series"
        :modern "A deferent with one epicycle is a sum of two uniform rotations; enough nested epicycles approximate any closed path, which is the discrete Fourier transform."
        :tex "z(t)=\\sum_k c_k\\,e^{i\\omega_k t}"}]
 :fields [{:field :mechanics-physics :label "Astronomy" :how "Ptolemy's model predicted planetary longitudes to a few degrees for fourteen centuries." :example "Retrograde loops of Mars from one epicycle of radius 39;30 parts of 60."}
          {:field :geometry :how "The theorem on the cyclic quadrilateral characterises concyclic points and gives the addition formulas." :example "Ptolemy's inequality makes the plane a Ptolemaic metric space."}
          {:field :logic-computing :label "Computing" :how "A precomputed table with interpolation is how numerical functions were evaluated until the computer, and how CORDIC and lookup tables still work." :example "The table's third column, a thirtieth of each difference, is linear interpolation."}
          {:field :mechanics-physics :label "Engineering" :how "Sums of rotating vectors are phasors and Fourier series." :example "Signal synthesis and the drawing of curves by chained epicycles."}]
 :tangents [{:series :physics :hook "From epicycles to Kepler's ellipse and Newton's gravity: why the circles worked so well."}
            {:series :erlangen :hook "The cross-ratio is invariant, Ptolemy's relation is its metric shadow on a circle."}]
